## Step 7: Validate with spec validate

After generating all spec artifacts, run validation to check the v1.2 JSON
files against the `afspec` format specification:

1. Run `spec validate <spec_name>` on the generated spec folder.
2. Review any errors or warnings in the output. The validator uses
   `afspec.validate()` which checks JSON schema conformance and
   cross-file integrity (e.g., requirement IDs referenced in test_spec.json
   must exist in requirements.json).
3. Fix all errors and warnings before presenting the spec to the user.
4. Re-run `spec validate <spec_name>` to confirm all issues are resolved.

The `afspec` library is the authoritative source for the v1.2 artifact
schemas. If validation reports schema errors, consult the afspec documentation
or source to understand the expected JSON structure.

This automated validation catches malformed JSON, missing required fields,
broken cross-references, and schema violations. It does NOT validate
semantic quality — use the manual checks in the completeness checklist below
for those.

---

## Completeness Checklist

After generating all spec artifacts and running `spec validate`, verify
the following before presenting to the user:

### PRD (prd.md)
- [ ] YAML frontmatter present with all required fields (spec_id, spec_name, title, status, etc.)
- [ ] `## Source` section present at the end

### Requirements (requirements.json)
- [ ] Valid JSON that passes `afspec.validate()`
- [ ] Every acceptance criterion `action` field contains the concept of SHALL
- [ ] All requirement IDs use `{spec_id}-REQ-{N}` format
- [ ] `introduction` and `glossary` fields are populated
- [ ] Every domain-specific term used in criteria appears in the glossary (manual check)
- [ ] No more than 10 requirements per spec (manual check)
- [ ] `correctness_properties` array has at least one entry
- [ ] `execution_paths` array has entries for each user-visible feature
- [ ] `error_handling` array references valid requirement IDs
- [ ] Every function whose output is consumed by a caller has a `return_contract` (manual check)

### Test Specification (test_spec.json)
- [ ] Valid JSON that passes `afspec.validate()`
- [ ] Every acceptance criterion has a test case entry
- [ ] Every correctness property has a property test entry
- [ ] Every edge case has an edge case test entry
- [ ] `smoke_tests` array has one entry per execution path
- [ ] Each smoke test lists `real_components` that must NOT be mocked (manual check)
- [ ] `coverage` object accounts for all requirements and properties

### Implementation Tasks (tasks.json)
- [ ] Valid JSON that passes `afspec.validate()`
- [ ] First task group has `"kind": "tests"`
- [ ] Every test spec entry is referenced by at least one subtask `test_spec_refs` (manual check)
- [ ] Each task group has a `verification` subtask
- [ ] `traceability` array lists every requirement ID
- [ ] No task group has more than 6 non-verification subtasks
- [ ] Last task group has `"kind": "wiring_verification"`

### Architecture (architecture.md — if present)
- [ ] `## Overview` and `## Architecture` sections are present
- [ ] References property IDs from requirements.json where appropriate
